# Part 8 – CI/CD (Continuous Integration & Continuous Deployment)

---

# 1. What is CI/CD?

CI/CD automates the process of building, testing and deploying applications.

Instead of manually deploying applications, changes flow automatically through a pipeline.

Typical flow:

```
Developer
    ↓
Git Repository
    ↓
Build
    ↓
Test
    ↓
Container Image
    ↓
Registry
    ↓
OpenShift Deployment
```

Benefits:

- Faster deployments
- Reduced manual effort
- Consistent releases
- Easier rollback
- Better quality

---

# 2. Continuous Integration (CI)

Continuous Integration focuses on integrating code changes frequently.

Typical activities:

- Source Code Checkout
- Compilation
- Unit Testing
- Static Code Analysis
- Artifact Generation

Example:

```
Developer

↓

Git Commit

↓

Jenkins

↓

Maven Build

↓

JAR Generated
```

---

# 3. Continuous Deployment (CD)

Continuous Deployment focuses on deploying the built artifact.

Typical activities:

```
JAR

↓

Container Image

↓

Registry

↓

OpenShift

↓

Pods
```

---

# 4. CI/CD in OpenShift

OpenShift supports automated builds and deployments.

Main components:

- BuildConfig
- ImageStreams
- Deployment
- Build Pods
- Image Registry

---

# 5. BuildConfig

BuildConfig tells OpenShift **how an image should be built.**

It defines:

- Source repository
- Build strategy
- Output image
- Build triggers

---

# 6. Build Strategies

OpenShift supports three major build strategies.

---

## Docker Strategy

Uses a Dockerfile.

Flow:

```
Git

↓

Dockerfile

↓

Docker Build

↓

Image

↓

Registry
```

Advantages:

- Complete control
- Enterprise standard
- Recommended by Trainer

---

## Source-to-Image (S2I)

Only source code is provided.

OpenShift automatically:

- Downloads dependencies
- Compiles application
- Creates image

Flow:

```
Source Code

↓

Builder Image

↓

Application Image
```

Advantages:

- Simple
- Minimal configuration

Limitations:

- Less customization
- Not ideal for complex enterprise applications

---

## Binary Strategy

Developer uploads

```
jar

war

zip
```

OpenShift creates image using binary artifact.

Flow:

```
JAR

↓

BuildConfig

↓

Container Image
```

Useful when another CI server already builds the application.

---

# 7. Which Strategy Should We Use?

Trainer Recommendation:

Docker Strategy

Reason:

- Better control
- Cleaner runtime image
- Enterprise friendly

---

# 8. Build Pods

Every OpenShift build runs inside a temporary Build Pod.

Flow:

```
Source Code

↓

Build Pod

↓

Container Image

↓

Registry
```

After build completion,

the Build Pod is deleted.

---

# 9. Image Registry

After successful build,

image is pushed to the Registry.

Flow:

```
Build Pod

↓

Registry

↓

Deployment
```

Images can later be pulled by Deployments.

---

# 10. ImageStreams

ImageStreams track image versions.

Benefits:

- Version management
- Automatic deployment triggers
- Easier rollback

Instead of directly using Docker images,

OpenShift tracks ImageStream versions.

---

# 11. Deployment Trigger

When a new image is available,

OpenShift automatically updates Deployments.

Flow:

```
New Image

↓

ImageStream Updated

↓

Deployment Triggered

↓

New Pods Created
```

---

# 12. Helm

Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes.

Instead of deploying many YAML files individually,

Helm packages them together.

Components:

```
Chart.yaml

values.yaml

templates/

charts/
```

---

# 13. Helm Chart

A Helm Chart contains everything required to deploy an application.

Typical structure:

```
Chart

↓

Deployment

↓

Service

↓

PVC

↓

ConfigMap

↓

Secret
```

---

# 14. values.yaml

Stores configurable values.

Example:

```
replicas: 3

image: receiver

tag: latest

port: 8080
```

Changing values.yaml avoids modifying multiple YAML files.

---

# 15. Helm Commands

Package Chart

```bash
helm package .
```

Install

```bash
helm install receiver .
```

Upgrade

```bash
helm upgrade receiver .
```

Uninstall

```bash
helm uninstall receiver
```

---

# 16. Source Cleanup

Trainer demonstrated why runtime images should remain small.

Avoid including:

- Source Code
- Git Repository
- Maven Cache
- Build Files
- Temporary Files

Production image should contain only:

- Application
- Runtime Libraries
- Configuration

